  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 AM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nAnother warm and soupy night out there with temperatures around the \narea in the 80s. For those in downtown KC, the urban heat island has \nkept the 90s around through midnight. Temperatures do continue to \ndescend through the night; however, they are only expected to bottom \nout in the low to mid 70s. The good news is the temperature trend \nthrough the week is downward, not a very steep downward, but down \nnonetheless. Also the pattern becomes more active introducing \nopportunities for showers and thunderstorms which will create rapid \nlocalized cooling followed by immediate swampy conditions behind the \nrain (for any daytime storms).\n\nSatellite imagery and H500 analysis shows the resident high \ncontinuing to sit over the Rocky Mountains; however, the eastward \nextent has waned and become less stout. This facilitates more \nshortwave perturbations progressing over the area and bringing \nmultiple potential rounds of precipitation over the next several \ndays.\n\nThe first looks to arrive this morning as a large thunderstorm \ncomplex maneuvers its way across SE Nebraska and northern KS. Short \nrange guidance suggests this complex makes its way toward the KC \nmetro and points south through the morning hours. This is supported \nby 1000mb-500mb thickness lines as well as elevated CAPE gradients. \nAdditional details from Corfidi vectors suggest storms will \npropagate eastward then dive southward as they enter western MO \npotentially making it all the way to the Ozarks as they decay \ntowards sunrise. There are several complications in the short term \nforecast primarily some residual boundaries from large scale activity \nto the east and potential weak frontogenesis across the area as the \nMCS approaches. These uncertainties will likely affect the strength \nand propagation of convection with consensus leaning toward overall \nweakening.\n\nRegardless of the presence of storms, the residual cloud cover is \nlikely to tamper the heat a bit across the area. NW flow establishes \nacross mid levels and above advecting relatively cooler and drier \nair into much of the region. High temperatures only reach the upper \n80s to 90s with heat index values around 100F. Unfortunately for \nthose towards to the I-44 corridor, heat index values remain near \n100-105F (advisory level); however, there is a potential that the \nMCS mentioned earlier will reach to that region resetting heat \nexpectations. It is prudent to see how far storms may propagate in \nthe effort to issue a more confident heat product. Additionally, \nfurther shortwaves are expected to transit the area today which may \nspark further convection later this afternoon and evening, although \ncoverage looks more favorable over the Ozarks than Central MO.\n\nThis pattern looks to continue through the rest of the week. The \ngood new is this means the decreasing temperatures trend continues \nwith highs lowering into the low 90s and heat indices settle around \nor below 100F. The ridge continues its hold over the Rockies and \nupper level shortwaves continue to flow over the region. Chances for \npop up showers and storms as well as development of nocturnal \nthunderstorms complexes continues. The subtle interactions of \nboundaries and mesoscale front make forecasting these systems \nsomewhat difficult on more course deterministic models. Consensus so \nfar forms these systems outside of the region and advances them into \nour area leaving local storm development to more pulse like pop-up \nstorms.\n\nDeterministic guidance suggests a larger scale change late week as a \ndeep trough is expected to dig out of interior Canada across the \neastern Plains. This presents the most substantial chances of \nconvection Friday and Saturday. Some uncertainties remain as small \ndeviations in the early track will result in larger scale variances \nin extended hour guidance. However, there is higher confidence that \nthis will significantly cool conditions with early forecast highs \nin the low to mid 80s.",
